    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey im studying for my security+ exam and im a bit stuck on password storage. if we are already using hashing to secure passwords, why do we actually need to use \"salting\" too? isn't hashing enough to keep them safe?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Hashing alone, while a crucial first step, is not enough to keep passwords safe from modern, highly optimized attacks. Salting is necessary because it prevents what's known as a rainbow table attack, which is a massive, pre-computed database of hashes.  A salt is a unique, random string added to each password *before* it is hashed, ensuring that even if two users have the same password, their stored hashes will look completely different. This makes cracking significantly harder for an attacker.",
